- How do you ensure exactly-once delivery across Outbox and message buses?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CQRS pattern in real-time collaborative apps - crucial for Miro's architecture where immediate UI feedback and data consistency are both required.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidate will explain: Command side receives shape update request, validates permissions, persists to database, emits ShapeUpdated event. Query side maintains read model for fast UI rendering. Event-driven architecture ensures eventual consistency across all connected clients.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Command
class UpdateShapePositionCommand {
  constructor(public shapeId: string, public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

// Command Handler
class ShapeCommandHandler {
  async handle(command: UpdateShapePositionCommand) {
    const shape = await this.repo.findById(command.shapeId);
    shape.updatePosition(command.x, command.y);
    await this.repo.save(shape);

    // Emit event for query side
    this.eventBus.emit(new ShapeUpdated(shape.id, shape.x, shape.y));
  }
}

// Query side optimized for UI
class ShapeReadModel {
  async getShape(shapeId: string) {
    return this.readDb.shapes.find(shapeId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle concurrent edits from multiple users?
- What happens if the command fails but the event was already emitted?
- How would you scale the read side for millions of users?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design distributed workflow with real systems concepts: saga orchestration, event sourcing, outbox pattern, and idempotent handlers under failure. Focuses on practical correctness and maintainability at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across multiple services
- Event sourcing and outbox integration
- Idempotent handlers using correlationId and event.id
- Compensating actions for partial failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator sketch
function onEvent(evt) {
  const key = `${evt.correlationId}:${evt.id}`;
  if (dedupe(key)) return;
  markHandled(key);
  switch (evt.type) {
    case 'OrderPlaced':
      enqueue('ReserveInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId, items: evt.items }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      enqueue('AuthorizePayment', { orderId: evt.orderId, amount: evt.total }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'PaymentAuthorized':
      enqueue('ShipOrder', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'OrderFailed':
      enqueue('ReleaseInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      enqueue('RefundPayment', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world cache invalidation across services affects consistency and latency; this tests practical patterns for multi-cache coherence.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside with per-key versioning
- Event-driven invalidation via a bus
- Redis vs Memcached nuances (pub/sub, TTLs, atomic ops)
- Thundering herd mitigation and stale-while-revalidate

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
function write(key, value){
  db.update(key, value);
  publisher.publish('invalidate', {key, version: newVersion(key)});
}
function read(key){
  const v = cache.getVersion(key);
  const val = cache.get(key);
  if (val && val.version === v) return val.value;
  const fresh = fetchFromDB(key);
  cache.set(key, {value: fresh, version: v+1}, ttl);
  return fresh;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift between Redis and Memcached?
- How would you test invalidation at scale?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design practical cache invalidation and explain trade-offs of Redis vs Memcached in a beginner context.

## Key Concepts

- cache-aside pattern
- tenant isolation in keys
- stampede mitigation with locks
- event-bus invalidation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating lazy reload with lock
async function getProduct(tid, pid) {
  const key = `tenant:${tid}:product:${pid}`
  let val = cache.get(key)
  if (val) return val
  const lock = `lock:${tid}:${pid}`
  if (redis.set(lock, 1, 'NX', 'PX', 1000)) {
    val = db.fetchProduct(tid, pid)
    cache.set(key, val, 300)
    redis.del(lock)
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getProduct(tid, pid)
  }
  return val
}
```

